      "title": "Eigenvalues and the Characteristic Equation",
      "teacher": "A matrix usually does two things to a vector — it stretches it AND it turns it. But a few very special directions refuse to turn. The matrix only stretches or shrinks them and leaves them pointing exactly the same way. Those directions are the eigenvectors, and how much they stretch is the eigenvalue. This is one of the most useful ideas in all of applied mathematics.",
      "explain": "For a square matrix A, a non-zero vector X is an <strong>eigenvector</strong> and the number λ is its <strong>eigenvalue</strong> if <strong>AX = λX</strong> — multiplying by the matrix has the same effect as multiplying by a single number. Rearranging gives (A − λI)X = O, which has a non-zero solution only when <strong>|A − λI| = 0</strong>. That is the <strong>characteristic equation</strong>.",
