      "worked": "<strong>Find the eigenvalues of A = [ 4 &nbsp;1 ; 2 &nbsp;3 ].</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1 — write A − λI:</strong><br>[ 4 − λ &nbsp; &nbsp;1 &nbsp; &nbsp; ]<br>[ 2 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 3 − λ ]<br><br><strong>Step 2 — set the determinant to zero:</strong><br>|A − λI| = (4 − λ)(3 − λ) − (1)(2) = 0<br><br><strong>Step 3 — expand:</strong><br>(4 − λ)(3 − λ) = 12 − 4λ − 3λ + λ² = λ² − 7λ + 12<br>So λ² − 7λ + 12 − 2 = 0 → <strong>λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0</strong><br><br><em>Cross-check using the shortcut:</em> trace = 4 + 3 = 7 and det = (4)(3) − (1)(2) = 10, so λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0 ✅ same equation.<br><br><strong>Step 4 — solve the quadratic:</strong><br>λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0 → (λ − 2)(λ − 5) = 0<br>→ <strong>λ = 2 and λ = 5</strong><br><br><strong>Step 5 — verify with the two free checks:</strong><br>Sum of eigenvalues = 2 + 5 = 7 = trace of A ✅<br>Product of eigenvalues = 2 × 5 = 10 = det A ✅<br><br>Both checks pass, so the eigenvalues are certainly <strong>λ = 2, 5</strong>.",
      "mistake": "Writing |A − λ| = 0 instead of |A − λI| = 0. You cannot subtract a plain number from a matrix — λ must be multiplied by the identity matrix first, so that it is subtracted from the <strong>diagonal entries only</strong>. The other frequent error is calling X = O an eigenvector; it never is.",
      "examAnswer": "Let A be a square matrix of order n. A non-zero column vector X is called an eigenvector of A, and the scalar λ the corresponding eigenvalue, if AX = λX. This relation may be rewritten as (A − λI)X = O, which is a homogeneous system of equations possessing a non-trivial solution if and only if the coefficient matrix is singular, that is, if and only if |A − λI| = 0. This determinantal equation is called the characteristic equation of A, and its roots are the eigenvalues of A. An n × n matrix has exactly n eigenvalues counted with multiplicity, the sum of the eigenvalues equals the trace of A and their product equals the determinant of A. Geometrically, an eigenvector represents a direction which the matrix merely scales by the factor λ without changing its orientation.",
