      "examAnswer": "Having obtained the eigenvalues of a square matrix A from its characteristic equation, the eigenvector corresponding to a particular eigenvalue λ is found by substituting that value into the homogeneous system (A − λI)X = O and solving it. Since |A − λI| = 0 by construction, the coefficient matrix is singular and the system therefore possesses infinitely many solutions, which yield a relation between the components of X rather than unique values. Any non-zero vector satisfying this relation is an eigenvector corresponding to λ, and every non-zero scalar multiple of it is also an eigenvector; the set of all such vectors together with the null vector forms the eigenspace of λ. Eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are always linearly independent.",
