      "teacher": "Here is a result that sounds almost like a magic trick. Every square matrix obeys its own characteristic equation. Put the matrix itself in place of λ, and the equation comes out as the zero matrix. And that trick hands you a genuinely quick way to find an inverse.",
      "explain": "The <strong>Cayley-Hamilton theorem</strong> states that <strong>every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation</strong>. So if the characteristic equation is λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0, then <strong>A² − 7A + 10I = O</strong> — where the constant term becomes a multiple of the identity matrix I, never a bare number. Multiplying this identity through by A⁻¹ gives a formula for the inverse.",
