      "realLife": "It is like discovering that a shopkeeper's own price list already contains the discount formula. You do not need any outside information — the matrix's own characteristic equation contains everything needed to invert it.",
      "worked": "<strong>Use the Cayley-Hamilton theorem to find A⁻¹ for A = [ 4 &nbsp;1 ; 2 &nbsp;3 ].</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1 — characteristic equation.</strong><br>trace = 4 + 3 = 7, &nbsp; det = (4)(3) − (1)(2) = 12 − 2 = 10<br>λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0<br><br><strong>Step 2 — apply Cayley-Hamilton (replace λ by A, constant by 10I):</strong><br><strong>A² − 7A + 10I = O</strong><br><br><strong>Step 3 — verify the theorem holds (optional but convincing).</strong><br>A² = [ 4 &nbsp;1 ; 2 &nbsp;3 ] × [ 4 &nbsp;1 ; 2 &nbsp;3 ]<br>entry (1,1) = 4(4) + 1(2) = 18<br>entry (1,2) = 4(1) + 1(3) = 7<br>entry (2,1) = 2(4) + 3(2) = 14<br>entry (2,2) = 2(1) + 3(3) = 11<br>So A² = [ 18 &nbsp;7 ; 14 &nbsp;11 ]<br>7A = [ 28 &nbsp;7 ; 14 &nbsp;21 ] &nbsp;and&nbsp; 10I = [ 10 &nbsp;0 ; 0 &nbsp;10 ]<br>A² − 7A + 10I = [ 18−28+10 &nbsp; 7−7+0 ; 14−14+0 &nbsp; 11−21+10 ] = [ 0 &nbsp;0 ; 0 &nbsp;0 ] = <strong>O</strong> ✅ The theorem checks out.<br><br><strong>Step 4 — multiply through by A⁻¹.</strong><br>A⁻¹(A² − 7A + 10I) = A⁻¹O<br>→ A − 7I + 10A⁻¹ = O<br><br><strong>Step 5 — make A⁻¹ the subject.</strong><br>10A⁻¹ = 7I − A<br><strong>A⁻¹ = (1/10)(7I − A)</strong><br><br><strong>Step 6 — compute it.</strong><br>7I = [ 7 &nbsp;0 ; 0 &nbsp;7 ]<br>7I − A = [ 7−4 &nbsp; 0−1 ; 0−2 &nbsp; 7−3 ] = [ 3 &nbsp; −1 ; −2 &nbsp; 4 ]<br><strong>A⁻¹ = (1/10) [ 3 &nbsp; −1 ; −2 &nbsp; 4 ] = [ 0.3 &nbsp; −0.1 ; −0.2 &nbsp; 0.4 ]</strong><br><br><strong>Step 7 — verify A · A⁻¹ = I.</strong><br>A · A⁻¹ = (1/10) [ 4 &nbsp;1 ; 2 &nbsp;3 ] × [ 3 &nbsp;−1 ; −2 &nbsp;4 ]<br>entry (1,1) = (4)(3) + (1)(−2) = 12 − 2 = 10<br>entry (1,2) = (4)(−1) + (1)(4) = −4 + 4 = 0<br>entry (2,1) = (2)(3) + (3)(−2) = 6 − 6 = 0<br>entry (2,2) = (2)(−1) + (3)(4) = −2 + 12 = 10<br>= (1/10) [ 10 &nbsp;0 ; 0 &nbsp;10 ] = [ 1 &nbsp;0 ; 0 &nbsp;1 ] = <strong>I</strong> ✅<br><br><em>Cross-check with the standard 2 × 2 formula:</em> A⁻¹ = (1/det)[ d &nbsp;−b ; −c &nbsp;a ] = (1/10)[ 3 &nbsp;−1 ; −2 &nbsp;4 ] — <strong>identical</strong> ✅",
      "table": {
        "headers": ["Step", "2 × 2 matrix A", "What you write"],
        "rows": [
          ["Characteristic equation", "λ² − (tr)λ + det = 0", "λ² − 7λ + 10 = 0"],
