      "mistake": "Writing the constant term as a plain number — 'A² − 7A + 10 = 0'. A number cannot be added to a matrix. It must be <strong>10I</strong>, and the right-hand side must be the null matrix <strong>O</strong>. Second most common error: forgetting to check det ≠ 0 before claiming an inverse exists.",
      "examAnswer": "The Cayley-Hamilton theorem states that every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation. That is, if the characteristic equation of a matrix A of order n is λⁿ + c₁λⁿ⁻¹ + … + cₙ = 0, then Aⁿ + c₁Aⁿ⁻¹ + … + cₙI = O, where I is the identity matrix of order n and O is the null matrix. The theorem provides a convenient method of computing the inverse of a non-singular matrix: multiplying the matrix equation throughout by A⁻¹ and rearranging expresses A⁻¹ as a polynomial in A. For example, if A satisfies A² − 7A + 10I = O, then multiplication by A⁻¹ gives A − 7I + 10A⁻¹ = O, whence A⁻¹ = (7I − A)/10. The method is applicable only when A is non-singular, that is, when the constant term of the characteristic equation, equal to ± det A, is non-zero.",
      "remember": "Put A in place of λ, and the constant becomes constant × I. Then multiply by A⁻¹ and rearrange.",
      "why": "It gives an inverse without computing a single cofactor, it reduces large matrix powers to simple expressions, and it is one of the most reliably asked questions in this whole unit.",
